%N A091284 Exponent of 2 in the prime factorization of prime(n)^8 - 1.
%C A091284 Also, exponent of 2 in -1+prime(n)^s, if s is an exponent of form 16k-8 (previous definition). - _Amiram Eldar_, Sep 21 2026
%C A091284 Exponents of 2 in -1+p^s if the exponent s(u) = (2^u)*k - 2^(u-1) comes from other sequence generated with s(u-1) exponent by adding 1 to terms of the "previous" sequence. E.g., s = 256k-128 needed an addition of 6 to the terms of A091282.
%H A091284 Amiram Eldar, <a href="/A091284/b091284.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%F A091284 a(n) = A091283(n) + 1 for n >= 2.
%F A091284 From _Amiram Eldar_, Sep 21 2026: (Start)
%F A091284 a(n) = A007814(A179645(n)-1).
%F A091284 Asymptotic mean: Limit_{m->oo} (1/m) * Sum_{k=1..m} a(k) = 6. (End)
%t A091284 IntegerExponent[Prime[Range[105]]^8 - 1, 2] (* _Amiram Eldar_, Sep 21 2026 *)
%o A091284 (PARI) a(n) = valuation(prime(n)^8-1, 2); \\ _Amiram Eldar_, Sep 21 2026
%Y A091284 Cf. A007814, A023506, A091282, A091283, A090739, A090740, A090129, A179645.
